 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Division by Zero**: In `project_cov3d_to_screen`, if $z_{cam} = 0$, the tangent calculation fails. A guard value (e.g., $10^{-6}$) should be used [G].
-*   **FOV Clamping**: The limit $1.3 \times \tan(\text{FOV})$ prevents extreme tangents that could cause numerical instability or incorrect projection for Gaussians near the frustum boundaries [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Low-Pass Filter**: The constant $0.3$ ensures the Gaussian is at least one pixel wide/high, preventing aliasing and numerical issues with very small covariances [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quaternion Normalization**: Quaternions must be normalized before converting to rotation matrices to ensure valid rotation matrices [G].
+- **Division by Zero**: In `project_cov3d_to_screen`, if $z$-coordinate in camera space is zero, division by zero occurs. A small epsilon should be added to $z$ [G].
+- **FOV Clamping**: The clamping limit of $1.3 \times \tan(\text{FOV})$ is critical to prevent extreme distortions for Gaussians near the frustum boundaries [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision**: Using `f32` (single precision) for large scenes may lead to precision issues in covariance computation. Double precision may be required for high-accuracy optimization [G].
+- **SH Dimension**: The SH matrix assumes a fixed dimension of 48 (degree 3). If higher degrees are used, the matrix size must be adjusted [S1, unofficial].
 
